    "lawCode": "Amd El L, generally",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S508A\n\nSPONSOR: MYRIE\n \nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the election law, in relation to\nauthorizing computer generated registration lists; in relation to the\nlist of supplies to be delivered to poll sites\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo update the Election Law to recognize that many counties have access\nto technology to employ computer generated registration lists at the\npoll sites, to make their use uniform, to update the list of supplies to\nbe delivered to the poll sites and to make certain gender neutral\ncorrections to the Election Law\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill amends Section 1-104 of the Election Law by adding a new\nsubsection (38) defining \"Computer generated registration list\" to be\nused in place of registration poll records to establish a person's\neligibility to vote in polling places on Election Day. The bill also\nrequires the State Board of Elections to promulgate minimum security\nstandards for any electronic device and any network or system to which\nthe electronic device is connected, as well as promulgate a list of\ndevices approved for use by boards of election.\n\nAdditionally, the bill requires the State Board of Elections to promul-\ngate minimum redundancy procedures to ensure a list of registration\nrecords is available to ensure voting continues if the electronic\ncomputer generated system becomes unavailable for any poll site od\nelection district.\n\nThe bill also sets forth the supplies to be provided to the polling\nsites for the new voting systems. The bill replaces various masculine\nreferences in the Election Law with gender neutral references. The bill\nalso clarifies the location where certain information pertaining to\nvoter information changes and challenges is to be recorded on the\ncomputer generated registration lists and the challenge report and the\nmanner in which same should be returned to the board of elections.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe advent of the new voting systems makes certain updates in the equip-\nment to be supplied to poll sites imperative. The computerization of\nregistration lists and their use at the poll sites is already authorized\nby Election Law § 5-506(3)(c). Guidance as to the use of computer gener-\nated lists must be provided. The requirement that the computer generated\nregistration list be in printed form is outdated, and fails to consider\nor enable the many administrative and Election Day benefits the technol-\nogy offers.\n\nIn 2019, the legislature enacted a bill to provide a period of early\nvoting prior to any primary, special or general election. Early voting\nis set to commence with the 2019 general election. This bill would\nprovide NYS counties the option to utilize electronic poll books for\nsuch elections, and thus equip them with an important tool to carry out\nthe administrative functions essential to the successful implementation\nof early voting in 2019 and beyond.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2016 - S.6581: Committed to Rules./A.8608A:Passed.\n2017/2018 - S.2788: Referred to Senate Elections/A.5547: Passed the\nAssembly\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone to the state and optional to the counties.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the first of January next succeeding the\ndate on which it shall have become a law.\n",
